{% extends '_article.jinja' %}
{% import 'components.jinja' as ui with context %}
{% from 'data/pages.jinja' import catalogue %}
{% set article = catalogue['architecture'] %}
{% set crumbs = [] %}
{% set plate_key = none %}
{% block article_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="vocabulary"><h2 id="vocabulary">Four nouns</h2>
<p>wireframe keeps each layer&rsquo;s vocabulary separate, so transport concerns do not leak into routing and routing does not leak into the domain.</p>
<dl class="wf-defs">
  <div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>The transport unit a <code>FrameCodec</code> cuts from the byte stream and writes back to it. It knows its own length and, perhaps, a correlation ID; it does not know what its payload means.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Fragment</dt><dd>A bounded slice of an oversized payload, with a header saying which message it belongs to and where it falls. Optional, and invisible above the transport.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Envelope</dt><dd>The routable wrapper decoded from a frame&rsquo;s payload: a message ID, an optional correlation ID, and the payload bytes. <code>Packet</code> is the trait; <code>Envelope</code> is the bundled type.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Message</dt><dd>The typed value inside an envelope&rsquo;s payload, encoded by the serializer. Bincode by default.</dd></div>
</dl>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="inbound"><h2 id="inbound">Inbound</h2>
<ol class="wf-steps">
  <li><strong>Accept.</strong> A worker accepts a TCP connection. The application was prepared once, before the server reported readiness, and every connection shares it.</li>
  <li><strong>First read.</strong> The server reads first, under a 10-second deadline by default: a preamble of up to 1&nbsp;KiB if one is configured, otherwise a first byte. Bytes read past it are replayed.</li>
  <li><strong>Decode frames.</strong> The codec cuts frames from the stream.</li>
  <li><strong>Deserialize.</strong> The serializer decodes an envelope, which must fill the frame exactly. A metadata-aware serializer can read routing fields first, but the framework still deserializes fully unless <code>should_deserialize_after_parse</code> returns false.</li>
  <li><strong>Reassemble.</strong> With fragmentation on, fragments carried in envelopes are rejoined into whole messages.</li>
  <li><strong>Assemble.</strong> With a message assembler installed, protocol-level multi-frame messages are joined.</li>
  <li><strong>Route.</strong> The message ID selects one route. No route: the message is logged and dropped.</li>
  <li><strong>Middleware and handler.</strong> The route&rsquo;s middleware stack runs, outermost first, around the handler.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="outbound"><h2 id="outbound">Outbound</h2>
<p>The reply goes back out through the middleware and is rebuilt as an envelope with the request&rsquo;s message ID and correlation ID. It is then fragmented if it is too large and fragmentation is on, and each piece is serialized, wrapped by the codec, passed to <code>before_send</code>, and encoded. The order is fixed, so each fragment is its own frame.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="state"><h2 id="state">Where state lives</h2>
<div class="wf-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Where state lives"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Scope</th><th scope="col">Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Application-wide</td><td>Values captured by handler and middleware closures. <code>app_data</code> stores values for the extractor building blocks, but nothing reads it while serving, so it never reaches handlers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Per connection</td><td>The value returned by <code>on_connection_setup</code>, handed to <code>on_connection_teardown</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Per middleware layer</td><td>Fields of the <code>Transform</code>, such as the counter in <a href="/wireframe/guides/middleware/#transform">the middleware guide</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Across connections</td><td><code>SessionRegistry</code>, holding weak push handles by connection ID. The application inserts them; nothing populates it automatically</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="not-yet"><h2 id="not-yet">What this release does not do</h2>
<p>A frank list, because every one of these appears somewhere in the upstream design documents. Several parts ship ahead of the runtime that will use them, and <a href="{{ source_base }}/docs/users-guide.md#building-blocks-not-yet-wired-into-the-server">the users&rsquo; guide</a> says how far each goes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Handlers do not return replies or take extractors, and <code>app_data</code> and request bodies do not reach them. Replies are written in middleware.</li>
  <li><code>middleware::from_fn</code> cannot be passed to <code>WireframeApp::wrap</code>.</li>
  <li>The routed server path sends one reply per request. Push queues, the connection actor, and streaming responses are not driven by it, and of the <code>WireframeProtocol</code> hooks it runs only <code>before_send</code>.</li>
  <li>Codec recovery policies and the <code>on_eof</code> hook are defined but not consulted.</li>
  <li>The read timeout does not close idle connections, and shutdown waits for them with no deadline.</li>
  <li>There is no built-in TLS.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <a href="/wireframe/roadmap/">roadmap</a> says what is in progress.</p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
